# I Ingested 6,356 Podcast Claims and Applied 104

<p class="deck">Building the pipeline was the easy part. Deciding what to ignore was not.</p>

<p class="byline">Casey Manos · 9 August 2026 · written with Claude Code against the live database</p>

For about two years I've run a personal research warehouse. It transcribes running podcasts, extracts every training claim the hosts make, resolves the studies they cite to primary sources, and indexes the result next to eight years of my own Garmin data so I can ask questions across both.

Today it holds **6,356 claims** from **626 episodes** across ten feeds, with **502** resolved studies. An extraction model scored **4,086** of those claims — 64% — as highly relevant to my goals.

I have applied **104**.

That gap is the whole story. Not because the pipeline failed: it works, it costs about eleven cents an episode, and it surfaces things I would never have found by listening. The gap is there because ingestion and judgment are different problems, and only one of them got cheap.

## What the thing actually is

The warehouse spans two projects and reads from two very different sources.

One side is personal: a Garmin export loaded into SQLite and queried through DuckDB — **2,941 activities from December 2017 through yesterday**, plus sleep, resting heart rate, and bodyweight, with training-load metrics pulled from intervals.icu alongside.

The other side is the corpus. A podcast episode is downloaded, transcribed with Whisper, and passed to a language model that pulls out every distinct claim: a protocol, a framework, a study citation, an anecdote. Each claim gets a structured record — what kind of claim it is, whether the host endorsed or doubted it, a timestamp back to the audio, a relevance score, and a link to the underlying paper when one can be resolved through PubMed, OpenAlex, or Semantic Scholar.

Both sides land in one indexed database with vector embeddings over the claims, so I can ask a question in plain language and get back the passages and the personal data that bear on it.

On top of that sit **findings** — 33 markdown documents that are mine, not the corpus's. A finding is an argued position: what I currently believe about a training question, why, which evidence supports it, and what would change my mind. They cite each other and they cite primary sources. When my own data contradicts one, the convention is to append a dated correction rather than quietly edit the old claim away.

## The number that surprised me

Adding a podcast is a config file. Ingesting the back catalogue of a new show is an afternoon. Across 112 logged runs the median cost is **$0.112 per episode**, and total recorded spend on the whole corpus is **$33.98**. Wall time is about four minutes per episode end to end.

So the marginal cost of *more* is approximately zero. Which is exactly the trap.

