Read the bottom two bars together. Of the 197 claims I actually put in front of myself, I applied 104 and rejected 93. That is roughly a coin flip — a healthy rate, and evidence the triage step is doing real work rather than rubber-stamping.

The brutal step is the one above it. The model marked 4,086 claims as highly relevant. I examined 197 of them. **The filter that mattered was not the relevance score. It was me having a specific open question.**

## Why the relevance score can't do this job

The scoring model is not wrong when it marks a claim relevant. A protocol for managing iron levels in endurance athletes genuinely is relevant to me. So is nearly every threshold-training framework, every heat-adaptation study, and every fueling guideline in the corpus. That's why 64% clears the bar.

But *relevant to my goals in general* is not the same predicate as *should change what I do this month*. The second one depends on things the corpus cannot see: what I already believe, what I've already tried, what my own data says about how I respond, and which questions are actually open right now.

That last one turned out to be the load-bearing piece. The system surfaces claims by matching them against **watches** — 26 declared pairs of (a finding, a topic I'm still uncertain about). A claim reaches me because it's similar to a question I have already written down as unresolved. Everything else stays in the database, searchable but unsurfaced.

This inverts the usual retrieval framing. The corpus is not a thing to be consumed. It's a thing to be *queried when you have a question*, and the scarce resource is well-formed questions, not documents.

## The docs said four podcasts. There were nine.

While gathering the numbers above I opened the architecture document I keep for exactly this purpose — the one that's supposed to let me return after a break and reload the system into my head. It is dated 3 May 2026 and describes four configured podcasts, roughly 290 episodes, about 3,500 claims, and 19 findings.

The live system had nine podcast configs, 626 episodes, 6,356 claims, and 33 findings. Every count was understated by 40 to 80 percent. Three months of drift.

Nothing broke, and that's the point worth sitting with: **the document degraded silently in exactly the way it was written to prevent.** It's a hand-maintained snapshot of a system that changes weekly, so it decays at whatever rate I forget to update it — and I forget at exactly the moments I'm busy shipping, which are the moments it changes most.

The findings don't have this problem, and the difference is instructive. A finding carries a `## Reproduce` section with the exact commands that generated its numbers. Anyone — including me in six months — can re-run them and see whether the claim still holds. The architecture doc has prose where those commands should be. I've since started treating any count in a document as suspect unless it ships with the query that produced it.

## What I'd tell someone building one

**Build the ingest, but budget for the judgment.** The pipeline is genuinely useful and genuinely cheap. It is also, in retrospect, about 20% of the work. The other 80% is maintaining a set of open questions precise enough that the corpus can answer them, and that part does not scale with compute.

**Keep the rejections.** The dismissed ledger has 93 entries and is the most under-rated artifact in the system. Without it I'd re-litigate the same claims every few months, because a claim I rejected looks identical to a claim I've never seen. Recording *no* is as valuable as recording *yes*, and it's the thing most retrieval systems throw away.

**Separate the corpus from your beliefs.** The single best structural decision was keeping findings as hand-written arguments the pipeline can never edit. The triage tool records that a claim should be integrated; it does not integrate it. That boundary is why I still trust the findings — they say what I think, not what the ingest happened to surface.

**Assume every number in your docs is stale.** Ship the query alongside the claim, or expect to be off by half within a quarter.

The honest summary is that I built a very efficient machine for producing 6,356 things I would not read, in order to find the 104 that changed my mind. I'd do it again — you cannot find the 104 without the 6,356, and no amount of upstream filtering identifies them in advance. But I'd stop describing the pipeline as the accomplishment. The pipeline is scaffolding. The ledger of what I applied, and what I refused, is the actual artifact.

